    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots into the established create-owner pipeline cleanly and mirrors the existing conventions precisely:\n\n- **Right unit, right triad.** `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` is a standalone guard function in the same package and with the same shape (`service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto, OwnerRepository) throws <Checked>`) as its siblings `CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique` and `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique`. The checked `CityAtCapacityException` and its `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler` match `DuplicateHouseholdException`/`DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler` one-for-one, and the handler reuses the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper rather than re-rolling the ProblemDetail body. A maintainer would find this entirely natural.\n\n- **Wiring is idiomatic.** The new step is inserted into `owners.POST.yml` as a guard before `build` (`checkHousehold \u2192 checkCityCapacity \u2192 build`), keeping all capacity/uniqueness guards together ahead of entity construction, and the escalation YAML follows the `officefloor/escalation/<FQCN>.yml` convention.\n\n- **No problematic duplication / no catch-all.** The inlined `findAll().stream()...count()` does echo the per-city counting in `AssignCustomerCode`, but the codebase deliberately keeps each guard self-contained (every sibling independently iterates `findAll()` rather than sharing a query helper, and there's no repository count-by-city method to reuse). Following that established pattern here is the consistent choice, not a smell. The `CITY_CAPACITY = 50` constant and the doc comment cross-referencing `AssignCustomerCode` for the case-insensitive comparison are appropriate.\n\nStructure is coherent, additive, and idiomatic \u2014 no changes needed.",
